Later, [[Ama-no-Uzume|Uzume]] and Sarutahiko fall in love and get married. Together, they found the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}}.<ref name="picken-2004a">{{Cite book |last=Picken |first=Stuart D. B. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BKAhUVqKFhgC&q=Uzume&pg=PA1 |title=Sourcebook in Shinto: Selected Documents |date=2004 |publisher=Greenwood Publishing Group |isbn=978-0-313-26432-0 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="roberts-2009a">{{Cite book |last=Roberts |first=Jeremy |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xdfgjV2kw6oC&q=Uzume |title=Japanese Mythology A to Z |date=2009 |publisher=Infobase Publishing |isbn=978-1-4381-2802-3 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="coulter-2013a">{{Cite book |last1=Coulter |first1=Charles Russell |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=VWxekbhM1yEC&dq=Ame-no-Uzume&pg=PT91 |title=Encyclopedia of Ancient Deities |last2=Turner |first2=Patricia |date=2013-07-04 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-135-96397-2 |language=en}}</ref>

The Kogo Shui says that this is the origin of a rite done by the Sarume.<ref name=":0" />

The Kojiki says that during her time in Ise, Uzume ensured the fish pledged their allegiance to Ninigi. The [[sea cucumber]], lacking a mouth, was unable to speak, so Uzume cut a mouth for it. This narrative depicts the Sarume no kimi as the initial recipients of offerings at Ise.<ref name=":0" />

*

== Mythological Background ==
The mythological narrative involving Ame no Uzume is central to understanding the Sarume's significance. According to Shinto myths, the world was plunged into darkness when Amaterasu, upset by her brother Susanoo's actions, secluded herself in a cave. To lure her out and restore light, the heavenly deities orchestrated a plan that included creating a sacred space with a mirror and jewels and performing rituals.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|53-68}}

Ame no Uzume took a central role in these efforts by performing an ecstatic dance that involved exposing herself, which amused the assembled deities and piqued Amaterasu's curiosity. As a result, Amaterasu emerged from the cave, marking the return of light to the world. This act by Ame no Uzume not only demonstrates the transformative power of joy and celebration but also establishes her as a deity of revelry and spontaneity.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|53-68}}

== Role in Shinto Rituals ==
The lineage of the Sarume to Ame no Uzume highlights their inherited right to perform specific Shinto rituals, particularly those associated with joy, music, and dance. Their performances are believed to embody the spirit of Ame no Uzume, evoking laughter and happiness to invite divine blessings. Through these rituals, the Sarume clan maintains a vital link between the divine and the human world, reflecting Shinto's emphasis on harmony and balance.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|53-68}}

== Cultural Significance ==
The Sarume's association with Ame no Uzume reflects broader themes in Shinto, such as the importance of ritual purity, the interconnection between deities and humans, and the power of natural and joyful expressions in maintaining cosmic order. The story of Ame no Uzume's dance underscores the belief in the transformative potential of art and celebration, an aspect that continues to influence Japanese cultural practices and festivals.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|53-68}}

