[[en:Draft:Sarume clan]]

The Sarume clan is a Japanese clan<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sarume Clan • . A History . . of Japan . 日本歴史 |url=https://historyofjapan.co.uk/wiki/sarume-clan/ |access-date=2023-10-13 |website=. A History . . of Japan . 日本歴史 |language=en-GB}}</ref>. They were founded by and descend from [[Ama-no-Uzume|Ame-no-Uzume]] and [[Sarutahiko Ōkami]].<ref name="picken-2004a" /><ref name="roberts-2009a" /><ref name="coulter-2013a" /><ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細|url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9422|access-date=2024-03-18|website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|language=ja}}</ref> They are sometimes identified with [[Miko]] or seen as their predecessors.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Kárpáti|first=János|date=2013|title=Music of Female Shamans in Japan|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/43290138|journal=Studia Musicologica|volume=54|issue=3|pages=225–256|issn=1788-6244}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Averbuch|first=Irit|date=1998|title=Shamanic Dance in Japan: The Choreography of Possession in Kagura Performance|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1178756|journal=Asian Folklore Studies|volume=57|issue=2|pages=293–329|doi=10.2307/1178756|issn=0385-2342}}</ref> Their female dancers did many dances that eventually seemed to fuse with the ceremonies of the Miko. Later on records of the Sarume disappeared.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=ge8cWl8OT3gC&pg=PA7&lpg=PA7&dq=sarume+miko&source=bl&ots=3-rirDOY9S&sig=ACfU3U2SXiNDAkXcLsQG-VAchhIu6H2z-A&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiqwd2xy_6EAxVJCTQIHX2kBrg4ChDoAXoECAIQAw#v=onepage&q=sarume%20miko&f=false|title=The Japanese Theatre: From Shamanistic Ritual to Contemporary Pluralism|last=Ortolani|first=Benito|date=1995|publisher=Princeton University Press|isbn=978-0-691-04333-3|language=en}}</ref>

They are said to have interbred with the [[Wani clan]]<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Akima |first=Toshio |date=1993 |title=The Origins of the Grand Shrine of Ise and the Cult of the Sun Goddess Amaterasu Ōmikami |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25790929 |journal=Japan Review |issue=4 |pages=141–198 |issn=0915-0986}}</ref> Which is believed to be related to [[Wani (dragon)|Wani dragons]], with Naniwa-neko Takefurukuma described as an ancestor. Marrying women of the Wani clan was believed to give the Emperor control over the sea.<ref name="Akima-1993a">{{Cite journal |last=Akima |first=Toshio |date=1993 |title=The Origins of the Grand Shrine of Ise and the Cult of the Sun Goddess Amaterasu Ōmikami |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25790929 |journal=Japan Review |issue=4 |pages=141–198 |issn=0915-0986}}</ref>{{Rp|page=149-150}} The wani clan had a similar religious role to the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}} as mediums, so many women of the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}} had husbands of the Wani clan perform ritual roles as substitutes for men of the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}}.<ref name="Akima-1993a" />{{Rp|page=168}}

