[[en:Draft:Sarume clan]]

The Sarume clan is a Japanese clan<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sarume Clan • . A History . . of Japan . 日本歴史 |url=https://historyofjapan.co.uk/wiki/sarume-clan/ |access-date=2023-10-13 |website=. A History . . of Japan . 日本歴史 |language=en-GB}}</ref>. They were founded by and descend from {{ill|Ama-no-Uzume|Ame-no-Uzume|en||simple}} and {{ill|Sarutahiko Ōkami|en||simple}}, and are known for their female ritualists.<ref name="picken-2004a" /><ref name="roberts-2009a" /><ref name="coulter-2013a" /><ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム">{{Cite web|title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細|url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9422|access-date=2024-03-18|website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|language=ja}}</ref> They are sometimes identified with {{ill|Miko|en||simple}} or seen as their predecessors.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Kárpáti|first=János|date=2013|title=Music of Female Shamans in Japan|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/43290138|journal=Studia Musicologica|volume=54|issue=3|pages=225–256|issn=1788-6244}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Averbuch|first=Irit|date=1998|title=Shamanic Dance in Japan: The Choreography of Possession in Kagura Performance|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1178756|journal=Asian Folklore Studies|volume=57|issue=2|pages=293–329|doi=10.2307/1178756|issn=0385-2342}}</ref> Their female dancers did many dances that eventually seemed to fuse with the ceremonies of the Miko. Later on records of the Sarume disappeared.<ref name="Ortolani-1995">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=ge8cWl8OT3gC&pg=PA7&lpg=PA7&dq=sarume+miko&source=bl&ots=3-rirDOY9S&sig=ACfU3U2SXiNDAkXcLsQG-VAchhIu6H2z-A&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiqwd2xy_6EAxVJCTQIHX2kBrg4ChDoAXoECAIQAw#v=onepage&q=sarume%20miko&f=false|title=The Japanese Theatre: From Shamanistic Ritual to Contemporary Pluralism|last=Ortolani|first=Benito|date=1995|publisher=Princeton University Press|isbn=978-0-691-04333-3|language=en}}</ref>

== Overview ==
The Sarume are known in Japanese mythology as descendants of Ame no Uzume, a deity celebrated for her pivotal role in the myth of Amaterasu, the sun goddess, retreating into the Ama-no-Iwato (heavenly rock cave). Their ancestral lineage underscores the clan's special privileges in performing imperial rituals within Shinto religion.<ref name="hardacre-2017a">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_Q81DQAAQBAJ|title=Shinto: A History|last=Hardacre|first=Helen|date=2017|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=978-0-19-062171-1|language=en}}</ref>{{rp|53-68}}

Sarume women were associated with {{ill|Kagura|en||simple}} dances being done during enthronement rites.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}

In the department of divinities there was this organization.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}}.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}

* The {{ill|Inbe clan|Inbe family|en||simple}} was responsible for managing the procuring materials for rituals and performing abstinence rites.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}} and distribution of tribute offerings within the Jingikan..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}} claiming descent from Ame no Futodama no Mikoto..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}
* Sarume family women were tasked with performing sacred dances..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}} Initially serving as seers and healers, their role evolved to include performing ritual dances, or kagura, during Enthronement Rites and court ceremonies..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}} claiming descent from the female Kami Ame no Uzume no Mikoto..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}
* The Tamatsukuri specialized in the creation of jewels..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}}
* The Kagamitsukuri were known for crafting bronze mirrors for use in shrine ceremonies..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}}
* The Urabe, considered to be of relatively low rank, carried out divination practices..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|35}} using tortoise shells and deer scapulae <ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}  later taking the surname Yoshida and claiming descent from Ame no Koyane..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}

* Nakatomi Clan: Later known as the Fujiwara, they specialized in reciting prayers to the Kami, with a divine lineage tracing back to Ame no Koyane no Mikoto..<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|25-26}}
[[ja:猿女君]]
== Mythical origins ==
[[File:Nihongi_by_Aston_djvu_109.jpg|left|thumb|Sarutahiko and Ame no Uzume, the founders of the clan]]
The mythological narrative involving Ame no Uzume is central to understanding the Sarume's significance. According to Shinto myths, the world was plunged into darkness when Amaterasu, upset by her brother Susanoo's actions, secluded herself in a cave. To lure her out and restore light, the heavenly deities orchestrated a plan that included creating a sacred space with a mirror and jewels and performing rituals.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|53-68}}

Ame no Uzume took a central role in these efforts by performing an ecstatic dance that involved exposing herself, which amused the assembled deities and piqued Amaterasu's curiosity. As a result, Amaterasu emerged from the cave, marking the return of light to the world. This act by Ame no Uzume not only demonstrates the transformative power of joy and celebration but also establishes her as a deity of revelry and spontaneity.<ref name="hardacre-2017a" />{{rp|53-68}}

In Ancient times Amaterasu ordered Uzume to accompany her grandson {{ill|Ninigi|en||simple}} on his journey to earth. They headed to ''{{ill|Ame-no-ukihashi|en||simple}}'' ("floating bridge of heaven") so they could head to earth but they were blocked by {{ill|Sarutahiko Ōkami|lt=Sarutahiko|en||simple}}. Uzume came and persuaded Sarutahiko to let Ninigi pass, in other versions of the story Uzume flirts with Sarutahiko.

Later, {{ill|Ama-no-Uzume|Uzume|en||simple}} and Sarutahiko fall in love and get married. Together, they found the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}}.<ref name="picken-2004a">{{Cite book |last=Picken |first=Stuart D. B. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BKAhUVqKFhgC&q=Uzume&pg=PA1 |title=Sourcebook in Shinto: Selected Documents |date=2004 |publisher=Greenwood Publishing Group |isbn=978-0-313-26432-0 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="roberts-2009a">{{Cite book |last=Roberts |first=Jeremy |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xdfgjV2kw6oC&q=Uzume |title=Japanese Mythology A to Z |date=2009 |publisher=Infobase Publishing |isbn=978-1-4381-2802-3 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="coulter-2013a">{{Cite book |last1=Coulter |first1=Charles Russell |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=VWxekbhM1yEC&dq=Ame-no-Uzume&pg=PT91 |title=Encyclopedia of Ancient Deities |last2=Turner |first2=Patricia |date=2013-07-04 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-135-96397-2 |language=en}}</ref>

The Kogo Shui says that this is the origin of a rite done by the Sarume.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" />

The Kojiki says that during her time in Ise, Uzume ensured the fish pledged their allegiance to Ninigi. The {{ill|sea cucumber|en||simple}}, lacking a mouth, was unable to speak, so Uzume cut a mouth for it. This narrative depicts the Sarume no kimi as the initial recipients of offerings at Ise.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" />

Later in their history they are said to have interbred with the {{ill|Wani clan|en||simple}}<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Akima|first=Toshio|date=1993|title=The Origins of the Grand Shrine of Ise and the Cult of the Sun Goddess Amaterasu Ōmikami|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25790929|journal=Japan Review|issue=4|pages=141–198|issn=0915-0986}}</ref> Which is believed to be related to {{ill|Wani (dragon)|lt=Wani dragons|en||simple}}, with Naniwa-neko Takefurukuma described as an ancestor. Marrying women of the Wani clan was believed to give the Emperor control over the sea.<ref name="Akima-1993a">{{Cite journal|last=Akima|first=Toshio|date=1993|title=The Origins of the Grand Shrine of Ise and the Cult of the Sun Goddess Amaterasu Ōmikami|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25790929|journal=Japan Review|issue=4|pages=141–198|issn=0915-0986}}</ref>{{Rp|page=149-150}} The wani clan had a similar religious role to the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}} as mediums, so many women of the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}} had husbands of the Wani clan perform ritual roles as substitutes for men of the {{ill|Sarume clan|ja|猿女君}}.<ref name="Akima-1993a" />{{Rp|page=168}} Their female dancers did many dances that eventually seemed to fuse with the ceremonies of the Miko. Later on records of the Sarume disappeared.<ref name="Ortolani-1995" />

