|alt=[[Sarutahiko Shrine]], the traditional shrine of the family}}The {{ill|Sarume clan|lt=Sarume clan|simple|User:Immanuelle/Sarume clan|en:draft|6=ja|7=猿女君}} was a Japanese clan<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sarume Clan • . A History . . of Japan . 日本歴史 |url=https://historyofjapan.co.uk/wiki/sarume-clan/ |access-date=2023-10-13 |website=. A History . . of Japan . 日本歴史 |language=en-GB}}</ref>. They were founded by and descend from [[Ama-no-Uzume|Ama-no-Uzume]] and {{ill|Sarutahiko Ōkami|en||simple|6=fr}}, and are known for their female ritualists.<ref name="picken-2004a" /><ref name="roberts-2009a" /><ref name="coulter-2013a" /><ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム">{{Cite web|title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細|url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9422|access-date=2024-03-18|website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|language=ja}}</ref> They are sometimes identified with {{ill|Miko|en||simple}} or seen as their predecessors.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Kárpáti|first=János|date=2013|title=Music of Female Shamans in Japan|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/43290138|journal=Studia Musicologica|volume=54|issue=3|pages=225–256|issn=1788-6244}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Averbuch|first=Irit|date=1998|title=Shamanic Dance in Japan: The Choreography of Possession in Kagura Performance|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1178756|journal=Asian Folklore Studies|volume=57|issue=2|pages=293–329|doi=10.2307/1178756|issn=0385-2342}}</ref> Their female dancers did many dances that eventually seemed to fuse with the ceremonies of the Miko. Later on records of the {{ill|Sarume clan|lt=Sarume|simple|User:Immanuelle/Sarume clan|en:draft|6=ja|7=猿女君|8=fr|9=Clan Sarume}} disappeared.<ref name="Ortolani-1995">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=ge8cWl8OT3gC&pg=PA7&lpg=PA7&dq=sarume+miko&source=bl&ots=3-rirDOY9S&sig=ACfU3U2SXiNDAkXcLsQG-VAchhIu6H2z-A&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiqwd2xy_6EAxVJCTQIHX2kBrg4ChDoAXoECAIQAw#v=onepage&q=sarume%20miko&f=false|title=The Japanese Theatre: From Shamanistic Ritual to Contemporary Pluralism|last=Ortolani|first=Benito|date=1995|publisher=Princeton University Press|isbn=978-0-691-04333-3|language=en}}</ref>

There is a {{ill|Sarume clan|lt=Sarume|simple|User:Immanuelle/Sarume clan|en:draft|6=ja|7=猿女君}} shrine which is a subshrine of [[Sarutahiko Shrine|Sarutahiko Shrine]] dedicated to them.<ref name="web.archive.org-2023a">{{Cite web|date=2023-03-31|title=Sarutahiko Shrine {{!}} Ise City Tourism Association|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230331032231/https://en.ise-kanko.jp/purpose/sarutahikojinja/|access-date=2024-03-19|website=web.archive.org}}</ref><ref name="web.archive.org-2022">{{Cite web|date=2022-12-01|title=Sarume Shrine {{!}} Ise City Tourism Association|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221201135853/https://en.ise-kanko.jp/purpose/sarumejinja/|access-date=2024-03-19|website=web.archive.org}}</ref><ref name="web.archive.org-2023b">{{Cite web|date=2023-12-09|title=Dedicated to Sarutahiko Okami, the Kami of Guidance｜みちひらきの大神 猿田彦神社|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231209214747/https://www.sarutahikojinja.or.jp/english/|access-date=2024-03-19|website=web.archive.org}}</ref><ref name="www.kankomie.or.jp">{{Cite web|title=What is Sarutahiko-JinjaShrine Shrine and Sarume-JinjaShrine shrines where the strongest blessings reside that are essential for visiting Ise? {{!}} Interview report {{!}} Kanko Mie (Kanko Mie)|url=https://www.kankomie.or.jp/report/1556?wovn=en|access-date=2024-03-19|website=www.kankomie.or.jp|language=en}}</ref>

